From 9e1dc0acecf79b217bfa06376bd2f16fd5b8f7a1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alexis Williams <alexis@typedr.at>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 19:00:52 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 36/36] tests: Skip what WebAssembly can't do, and fix what it
 showed up

Where an expectation can't hold on WebAssembly, skip the subtest, or
the part of it, with the reason:
- musl takes any locale name but has no locale data: collate's lists
  that sort differently under en_US, option-context's decimal comma,
  date's Polish month names, and gdatetime's en_GB formats, de_DE month
  names and eras.
- WasmFS has no sockets, and Emscripten can't spawn or fork processes,
  open a pseudo-terminal, parse an IPv6 scope ID or list network
  interfaces; nothing can send it a Unix signal.
- WasmFS keeps file times as a double count of milliseconds, checks no
  directory's search permission, gives pipes no capacity, and takes an
  empty file or device for unreadable in poll().
- Time zones come from Intl, so no TZif file is read by path.
- gio-tool's launch command needs desktop applications and spawning,
  and WebAssembly's content types know no MIME types.

Where musl's output is right but differs, expect it: strftime() pads
years to four digits and has no %k, %l or %P, and iconv() reports a
character the target lacks once it has consumed the input. A
WebAssembly GMainContext polls no wakeup fd unless it is ownerless, so
mainloop expects one fewer fd. The program is named after the .js
script Node runs.

Now that WebAssembly builds glib-unix and GIO's Unix streams, run the
subtests that use them: pollable's pipe, file, /dev/null and converter
tests, mainloop's and closure's fd sources, and the gio-unix-2.0
pkg-config test. convert and converter-stream check that a replaced
character is found also when the conversion runs out of output room or
ends in an incomplete character.

Fix the tests themselves where they relied on more than they should:
- filenamecompleter connected g_main_loop_quit() to a signal with
  another signature, which traps, and expected completions in the order
  one file system lists them;
- gdbus-peer's credentials test assumed credentials on every Unix;
- socket-listener's mocks and messages-low-memory's malloc() look up the
  real function with dlsym(RTLD_NEXT), which finds nothing in a static
  WebAssembly link;
- glib/tests never told the Python tests to run their programs with
  Node, and those tests expected abort() to end Node with SIGABRT.

Workarounds keep tests running: test-printf's reader waits for its
writer, since Emscripten's pipe reads never block; mainloop's nfds test
gives its file a byte, and its unix-fd-priority test and closure's fd
test read a pipe holding a byte instead of /dev/random or /dev/null,
since WasmFS's poll() only takes a file or device for readable while it
holds data; and fileutils' stdio-wrappers test works in a temporary
directory, since chmod() on WasmFS's Node backend doesn't reach the
host. convert and converter-stream also check stateful charsets: a
byte order mark and ISO-2022-JP's shift state across calls and full
output, and a fallback between kanji in ISO-2022-JP.

Only test sources and test build files change.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [Wasm-specific]
